"A consensus protocol diagram shows how replicas agree on ordered state changes under a named algorithm and failure model.\n\nUse when: Explain leader election or replicated-log commitment. Review a protocol trace containing delays or failures.\n\nAvoid when: A simple replication topology answers the question without protocol detail.\n\nVisual grammar: Replica lanes show roles such as leader, follower, or candidate. Messages identify terms, indices, acknowledgments, or votes as the algorithm requires. Commit markers distinguish replicated entries from applied or merely proposed entries.\n\nBuild: Name the algorithm, replica membership, and modeled failure assumptions. Choose one election or append scenario. Draw messages and role changes in causal order. Reserve side space for term and log annotations, keeping commit markers away from message text.\n\nCheck: Algorithm-specific election and commit rules are respected. A majority is computed against the correct membership. Replicated, committed, and applied states remain distinct. Delayed messages and failure intervals do not intersect replica labels.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming receipt by one follower proves commitment. Generalizing a Raft-specific rule to every consensus algorithm.\n\nExample: A Raft leader appends an entry, obtains the required replica acknowledgments, and advances its commit index.\n\nAlternatives: Replication topology, Causal-order diagram."
